Concretely, given d as the number of dependency labels, which indicate the grammatical relations between dependents and their heads, we transform dependency trees into polynomials recursively based on two variable sets: X = {x₁, x₂, ... x_d} and Y = {y₁, y₂, ... y_d}. Considering a node with Label l as nˡ, its corresponding polynomial is represented as:

$$
P(n^l) =
\begin{cases}
x_l, & n^l \text{ is leaf,} \\
y_l + \prod_{i=1}^k P(n_i), & n^l \text{ is non-leaf,}
\end{cases}
$$

where n₁, ..., nₖ are all child nodes of nˡ if it is non-leaf. Then, given a root node r, we obtain the polynomial representing the whole tree by computing P(r). A detailed demonstration from Liu et al. (2022) can be found in Appendix A.

Compared to other algorithms mentioned above, Polynomial Distance (Liu et al., 2022) is convenient to implement and relatively time-saving to run. So, in this work, we adopt Polynomial Distance between dependency trees to measure the syntactic similarity. [...] Then, we compute the distance between two polynomials (P and Q) as: $$
d(P, Q) = \frac{\sum_{s \in V_P} \min_{t \in V_Q} \| s - t \|_1 + \sum_{t \in V_Q} \min_{s \in V_P} \| s - t \|_1}{|V_P| + |V_Q|}
$$ [...] For each test sample, we compute its Polynomial Distance from each instance of the example database on the source side, and then select the top-k most similar examples.
